## 4. Program policy is a hard constraint, not a style preference

Bug bounty programs very commonly have written policy language along the
lines of *"do not exfiltrate data under any circumstances"* — treat this as
binding on every step in §3, not as generic good judgment you're free to
override when a finding feels compelling. Concretely:

- Never retrieve actual record/document *content* — only structure, counts,
  or existence, per §3.2.
- Never attempt an action that creates real state changes (new accounts,
  written records, modified configuration) on a live system to "prove" a
  point, unless the program has explicitly told you to.
- If uncertain whether a specific next test crosses this line — the
  standard, sanctioned answer in most programs' own written policy is to
  **ask the program**, not to unilaterally decide and proceed. Programs that
  publish this instruction mean it literally; use it.

