## 5. Classifying the finding correctly

Getting the CWE and severity narrative right the first time avoids a
credibility-damaging back-and-forth with triage. Three CWEs come up
repeatedly for this class, and picking the wrong one is the single most
common mistake:

| CWE | When it applies | Common mistake to avoid |
|---|---|---|
| **CWE-798** (Use of Hard-Coded Credentials) | The credential itself is the actual bearer of authorization, and it's over-scoped/non-expiring/shouldn't have been provisioned that broadly for client-side use. | Don't file this for an *expected-public* identifier (§2, point 2) — that's not the defect. |
| **CWE-284** (Improper Access Control) | The credential is expected-public, but the *service-side authorization rules* layered on top of it are misconfigured to allow more than intended (e.g. default/test-mode rules left in production). | Don't blame "the key was exposed" in the summary — the key being public is normal for this class; lead with the rules misconfiguration instead. |
| **CWE-200** (Exposure of Sensitive Information) | Almost always the *consequence*, not the root cause — cite it as secondary, supporting the CWE-798 or CWE-284 primary classification, not as the finding's headline. | Filing this as the primary/only classification tends to invite "so what's actually broken?" pushback — see §6. |

---

## 6. CVSS reasoning worth getting right — and defending

**Score what the confirmed defect structurally guarantees, not just what you
chose to retrieve.** This is the most important, most defensible move
available in this whole methodology, and it's worth internalizing as a
general principle rather than a one-off trick:

If a platform's authorization model has a **documented cascading/inheritance
property** (a permission granted at a broader scope automatically applies to
everything narrower beneath it, with no mechanism to restrict it back down at
a deeper level), then confirming the defect exists *at the broad scope* is
sufficient — by the platform's own architecture — to establish impact across
the full scope it covers, **even if you deliberately read very little of
it.** This is a *deterministic* argument, not a probabilistic "an attacker
would probably go further" one: don't state it as the latter if you can
establish the former. Verify the cascading/inheritance behavior against the
specific platform's own documentation before citing it — don't assume one
vendor's model transfers to another's.

Conversely, don't claim impact you have no basis for: keep integrity/
availability impact explicitly marked "not tested" when you haven't (and
couldn't safely, per §3.3) tested write access, rather than letting a
generically-severe-sounding write-up imply more than was confirmed.

A reasonable baseline vector shape for "unauthenticated read access to
production data via an over-scoped/misconfigured credential, read-only,
network-reachable, no user interaction": `AV:N/AC:L/PR:N/UI:N/S:U/C:{L or H,
per the cascading argument above}/I:N/A:N`. Don't manufacture false
precision trying to match a specific number a reviewer or tool suggested —
show your vector and reasoning, note where you land, and defer the final
number to the program's own triage rather than contesting a fractional
difference.

