## 8. Reporting checklist

- [ ] Correct CWE (§5) — root cause (798 or 284) as primary, 200 as
      consequence only.
- [ ] Explicit statement of what *was* and *was not* retrieved/tested,
      including an explicit "what I did not do and why" section referencing
      program policy where applicable (§4).
- [ ] Verification evidence via the least-invasive technique available for
      that service (§3.1/§3.2) — introspection/self-describing endpoint
      preferred over empirical structure-only reads where both exist.
- [ ] CVSS vector with reasoning shown, including the cascading/inheritance
      argument (§6) where it applies, and explicit "not tested" markers for
      any impact dimension you didn't verify.
- [ ] If the same misconfiguration appears across multiple instances sharing
      an obvious common template/pipeline — one report, not one per instance
      (check the specific program's own policy language on this; many state
      it explicitly).
- [ ] Any dead/non-functional lead found during the same recon pass (e.g. a
      credential pointing at a decommissioned service) — worth a one-line
      footnote for completeness, not its own report.
- [ ] Evidence: full request/response (headers + body) for every verification
      call, redacting only fields that are plausibly sensitive on their own
      merits (an internal description/label field, for instance) — not the
      credential itself if it's an expected-public identifier type (§2).
